  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nDiurnal cumulus clouds occurring this afternoon will continue, \nand some of these clouds may produce sprinkles, particularly in \nthe Minnesota Arrowhead. Breezy northwest winds will gradually \nlighten toward evening as surface high pressure settles over the\nregion. For tonight, mostly clear skies and light winds will \nallow for radiational cooling. Patchy fog will develop after \nmidnight, with best chances for areas of dense fog in the Iron \nRange.\n\nSunday will bring pleasant and quiet weather across the area with\nmostly sunny skies as surface high pressure remains in control.\nCool low temperatures in the 40s are expected overnight. Moving\ninto Monday, shortwave ridging aloft will maintain dry conditions\nthrough most of the daylight hours. Moisture will begin returning\nlater in the day as an upper-level low approaches from the\nnorthwest.\n\nElevated CAPE with a warm front will lead to chances for showers\nand storms Monday night into early Tuesday. The environment will\nbe less conducive for convection Tuesday morning as warm, moist air\ncontinues to be advected into the region. Tuesday afternoon and\nevening, a cold front will move through and will help \nconvection redevelop for eastern portions of the Northland. \nSome storms could also be severe as instability maximizes with \nsufficient wind shear ahead of the front.\n\nShowers and non-severe thunderstorms will remain possible into\nWednesday as cyclonic flow lingers behind the departing system.\nPrecipitation will gradually taper off from west to east as the\nlow pressure area shifts into the Great Lakes region.\n\nHigh pressure and northwest flow aloft return for the second half\nof the work week, bringing a return to dry conditions across the\nNorthland for Thursday and Friday. Precipitation chances remain\nbelow fifteen percent through the end of the period.",
